Let's plan document structure:

# Community Energy Deployment — Reviewable Package
Metadata: date (2026-11-05?), repo revision, status: DRAFT FOR DECISION, prepared by principal, evidence cut-off.

## 0. Decision summary
- Decision requested: authorize pre-award verification and RFQ, not construction.
- Current recommendation: Site A shortlist Kestrel + Solaris; Halcyon excluded at A. Site B: no eligible vendor; hold. No expenditure approved.
- Five gating conditions.

## 1. Scope, basis, and evidence rules
- Sources list, evidence classes (Measured/Declared/Assumption/Unverified/Missing).
- Package is decision-support; no cost data exists.
- Limits: quantities, currency, site max temp, installation, grid interconnection absent.

## 2. Technical capacity assessment
### 2.1 Method / pass-fail criteria
- Cold design: unit min_c <= site design min. (Careful: min_c is lower operating limit; to be safe need margin? It says design minimum -12; unit min -15/-20 pass. No margin requirement stated.)
- Width: unit_width <= max admissible.
- Upper temp: unit max_c >= site design max; site design max missing.
- Coastal: current 2026 cert + coastal-rated warranty for B.
### 2.2 Site A matrix
Table with Kestrel, Solaris, Halcyon.
Kestrel: -20 ≤ -12 pass (8 K margin); 900 ≤ 1200 pass (300 mm); upper unknown; overall "technically eligible, subject to upper-temp confirmation".
Solaris: -15 ≤ -12 pass (3 K); 800 ≤ 1200 pass (400 mm); overall eligible.
Halcyon: -10 > -12 FAIL by 2 K; excluded.
Note width margin.
### 2.3 Site B matrix
- cold: all pass.
- coastal: all fail current evidence (Kestrel none, Solaris none, Halcyon expected only).
- width: unverified assumption 1200; if true all fit. But no measurement, so conditional.
- Overall: none eligible today.

Maybe a "derived margins" table:
Kestrel A cold margin 8 K, width margin 300 mm.
Solaris A cold margin 3 K, width margin 400 mm.
Halcyon A cold margin -2 K (deficit), width margin 350 mm.
Site B cold margins: 16, 11, 6 K.
Halcyon at B: min -10 vs -4 => 6 K margin, so thermal fine. Width 850 vs assumed 1200 => 350 mm if assumption holds. But cert missing.

## 3. Vendor compliance assessment
Table: vendor, warranty, coastal cert evidence, Site A technical, Site B technical, lead, compliance status.
- Kestrel: 5y; none; A pass (cold/width) but upper TBD; B fail coastal; "Conditionally eligible A / not eligible B".
- Solaris: 7y; none; A pass; B fail; same.
- Halcyon: 10y; expected only; A fail cold; B fail coastal; "Not eligible A / not eligible B".
Provenance notes: warranty duration is declared, not verified contract; longer warranty doesn't cure cert. 2024 column not current 2026 cert.
Maybe "certificate acceptance criteria": issuer, cert number, issue/expiry dates covering 2026, model/series match, coastal/salt-fog standard, warranty linkage, not expired. Also require warranty document explicitly coastal-rated.

## 4. Cost / budget model
- Status: unpriced. No prices, quantities, currency, tax, freight, installation, or budget authority in repo.
- Cost breakdown structure table with element, basis, source/status.
  - Unit supply: N_s × unit price (quote required)
  - Freight/logistics
  - Site works/foundations
  - Installation/labour
  - Electrical/grid interconnection
  - Commissioning/testing
  - Coastal certification/warranty premium
  - Spares
  - Owner's costs (PM, legal, permitting)
  - Contingency
  - Escalation/FX
  - OPEX: service, warranty, insurance
- Formulas:
  Capex_s = N_s*(P_s + F_s + I_s + C_s) + W_s + O_s
  Program = Σ + contingency + escalation
  LCOE? Not enough energy yield data. Better avoid or state cannot compute.
- Budget decision: no budget can be set; zero commitment. Pre-award authorization cap TBD by approver. Maybe recommend "not to exceed" but no number.
- Cost risk: lead-time-linked escalation, cert premium, redesign if width < unit.

Could make "minimum data set required before budget approval" list. This is important.

## 5. Implementation schedule
- Only sourced durations: lead_weeks 8/10/12. All other durations TBD.
- Baseline relative schedule with gates:
  W0: package acceptance; authority gate for pre-award; RFQ; Site B survey; cert requests.
  W0–W2: verification window (2 weeks is a planning assumption, not source).
  G1 at W2: width measured, current cert on file, quotes, authority.
  PO at G1.
  Equipment availability: Kestrel W10, Solaris W12, Halcyon W14 (if eligible; Halcyon not for A).
  Install/commissioning: TBD after delivery.
- Site A can proceed independently at G1, using Kestrel/Solaris. Site B cannot PO until cert gate.
- Critical path: Site B cert + survey, then vendor lead time. If cert not by G1, Site B slips week-for-week; no schedule can be committed.
- Maybe table "Earliest equipment availability (indicative, excludes installation)".
- Note: lead weeks are unit lead, not installation; no weather/season data.

## 6. Risk and uncertainty review
Risk register table: ID, risk, likelihood/impact, evidence, mitigation, owner, trigger.
Use qualitative H/M/L, no fake probabilities. Maybe 12 risks.
- R1 Halcyon cold shortfall (avoid)
- R2 No current coastal cert (Site B block) — high
- R3 Site B width assumption — high
- R4 Site max temp unknown — medium
- R5 No cost basis / budget — high
- R6 No spend authority — high
- R7 Certificate scope/validity mismatch — medium
- R8 Warranty not coastal-rated even if cert — high
- R9 Lead time and price validity — medium
- R10 quantities/scale unknown — medium
- R11 installation/commissioning/interconnection unknown — medium
- R12 vendor claim drift / stale docs — medium
- R13 single vendor dependency — medium
- R14 weather/seasonal — low/medium

Uncertainty classification table:
- Known knowns (temps, width limits, lead times, warranty years)
- Known unknowns (site B width, site max temps, quantities, costs, cert, install duration)
- Unknown unknowns / exclusions (permitting, grid, geotech, etc.)

## 7. Integrated decision
- Decision matrix scoring (technical pass/fail first, then commercial only if pass). No weighted scoring because no costs.
- Site A: eligible Kestrel/Solaris; no preference on cost; Kestrel shorter lead, Solaris longer warranty. Recommend dual RFQ; do not select until price/authority.
- Site B: no award; conditional path. Halcyon's 10-year warranty is not a substitute for cert; it may become eligible only with current 2026 cert; Kestrel/Solaris also may qualify if they produce cert.
- Overall recommendation: "HOLD — CONDITIONAL GO FOR VERIFICATION ONLY". No expenditure approval.
- Conditions precedent:
  C1 written delegated authority and approved budget envelope
  C2 measured Site B width
  C3 current 2026 coastal cert on file + coastal warranty terms for B
  C4 priced quotes with quantities/currency/tax/freight/validity
  C5 site max temperature confirmation
  C6 installation/commissioning plan and duration
- Decision record table: item, decision, authority, date.
- Next review: upon inbox updates / Gate G1.

## 8. Evidence ledger / source register
Table with claim, class, source, treatment.
This could also be section 1 or appendix.

## Appendix A: assumptions register
## Appendix B: open requests / RFI questions
## Appendix C: change log

Need be careful with dates. Current runtime says run-2026-11-05. Use "2026-11-05" as package date, but maybe no need. Git commit hash base 9b8a94e.

